our global offices and research labs. L31: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL32: \nL33: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. L34: \nL35: cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L36: \nL37: Research\nL38: ## Explore our many areas of focus\nL39: \nL40: cite1†Explore all research areas L41: \nL42: Applied AI & sciences\nL43: \nL44: cite2†Earth AI cite3†Health AI cite4†Science AI cite5†Sustainability & crisis resilience L45: \nL46: Foundational ML & algorithms\nL47: \nL48: cite6†Algorithms & theory cite7†Information retrieval cite8†Machine intelligence cite9†Machine perception cite10†Natural language processing L49: \nL50: People, systems & quantum AI\nL51: cite11†Human-computer interaction and visualization cite12†Networking cite13†Quantum AI cite14†Responsible AI cite15†Anti abuse cite16†Software engineering cite17†Software systems L52: \nL53: Learn More\nL54: \nL55: cite18†Publications cite19†Projects L56: \nL57: Resources\nL58: ## Building a collaborative ecosystem\nL59: \nL60: cite20†Datasets Access high-quality datasets to accelerate your research. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L61: \nL62: cite30†Conferences & events L63: \nL64: Careers\nL65: ## Shaping the future together\nL66: \nL67: cite23†See all programs L68: \nL69: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L70: \nL71: cite31†Blog L72: \nL73: About\nL74: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL75: \nL76: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L77: \nL78: cite32†Google Research L79: \nL80: cite33†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite34†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI†deepmind.google cite35†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google L81: \nL82: [Button: Research ]\nL83: \nL84: [Button: Resources ]\nL85: \nL86: cite30†Conferences & events L87: [Button: Careers ]\nL88: \nL89: cite31†Blog L90: \nL91: [Button: About ]\nL92: \nL93: [Input: Search]\nL94: \nL95: Search\nL96: \nL97: cite36†Image: Hero image showing privately generated synthetic data†storage.googleapis.com L98: # A picture's worth a thousand (private) words: Hierarchical generation of coherent synthetic photo albums\nL99: \nL100: October 20, 2025\nL101: \nL102: Weiwei Kong, Software Engineer, and Umar Syed, Research Scientist, Google Research\nL103: \nL104: We introduce a method for generating differentially private synthetic photo albums that uses an intermediate text representation and produces the albums in a hierarchical fashion.\nL105: ## Quick links\nL106: cite37†Differential privacy†en.wikipedia.org (DP) provides a powerful, mathematically rigorous assurance that sensitive individual information in a dataset remains protected, even when a dataset is used for analysis. Since DP’s cite38†inception nearly two decades ago†link.springer.com , researchers have developed differentially private versions of myriad data analysis and machine learning methods, ranging from calculating simple statistics to cite39†fine-tuning complex AI models .\nL107: However, the requirement for organizations to privatize every analytical technique can be complex, burdensome, and error-prone.\nL108: Generative AI models like cite40†Gemini†gemini.google.com offer a simpler, more efficient solution. Instead of separately modifying every analysis method, they create a single private synthetic version of the original dataset. This synthetic data is an amalgamation of common data patterns, containing no unique details from any individual user.\nL109: By using a differentially private training algorithm, such as cite41†DP-SGD†arxiv.org , to fine-tune the generative model on the original dataset, we ensure the synthetic dataset is both private and highly representative of the real data. Any standard, non-private analytical technique or modeling can then be performed on this safe (and highly representative) substitute dataset, simplifying workflows.\nL110: DP fine-tuning is a versatile tool that is particularly valuable for generating high-volume, controlled datasets in situations where access to high-quality, representative data is unavailable.\nL111: Most published work on private synthetic data generation has focused on cite42†simple outputs†arxiv.org like short text passages or individual images, but modern applications using multi-modal data (images, video, etc.) rely on modeling complex, real-world systems and behaviors, which simple, unstructured text data cannot adequately capture.\nL112: We introduce a new method for privately generating synthetic photo albums as a way to address this need for synthetic versions of rich, structured image-based datasets. This task presents unique challenges beyond generating individual images, specifically the need to maintain thematic coherence and character consistency across multiple photos within a sequential album. Our method is based on translating complex image data to text and back.\nL113: Our results show that this process, with rigorous DP guarantees enabled, successfully preserves the high-level semantic information and thematic coherence in datasets necessary for effective analysis and modeling applications.\nL114: ## How (and why) our method works\nL115: \nL116: Our method differs from most other approaches to generating private synthetic image data in two major respects: (1) we use an intermediate text representation and (2) we generate the data hierarchically.\nL117: \nL118: Here’s how it works:\nL119:   1. We generate a structured text representation of each original album, replacing each photo in the album with an AI-generated detailed text caption, and also using an AI model to produce a text summary of each album.\nL120:   2. We then privately fine-tune a pair of large language models to produce similar structured representations. The first model is trained to generate album summaries, and the second model is trained to generate individual photo captions based on an album summary.\nL121:   3. We use the models to generate structured representations of photo albums in a hierarchical manner. For each photo album, we first generate a summary of the album, and then using that summary as context, we generate a detailed text caption of each photo in the album.\nL122:   4. The generated structured representations are then converted into sets of images using a text-to-image AI model.\nL123: cite43†Image: Illustration of our method for generating synthetic photo albums.†storage.googleapis.com L124: \nL125: Illustration of our method for generating synthetic photo albums.\nL126: Generating text as an intermediate step towards generating images has a number of advantages. First, text generation is the main strength of a large language model. Second, text summarization is inherently privacy enhancing, since describing an image by text is a lossy operation, so synthetic photos are unlikely to be exact copies of the originals, even when differential privacy is not enabled.\nL127: Finally, generating images is far more costly than generating text, so by first generating text, we can filter albums based on their content before expending resources to produce the images in which we are most interested.\nL128: Our hierarchical generation strategy ensures that the photos in each album are internally consistent, since each photo caption in an album is generated with the same album summary as context. Also, generating the structured representations in two steps (first the album summaries, and then the photo captions) preserves significant computational resources relative to generating each representation in one shot.\nL129: Since training cost scales quadratically with context length (due to cite44†self-attention†arxiv.org ), training two models with shorter contexts is far less costly than training a single model with a long context.\nL130: It may seem that describing images with words is too lossy an operation to preserve any interesting characteristics of the original images, but a simple demonstration (without differential privacy, to allow for side-by-side comparison) illustrates the power of this approach. In the figure below, we prompted Gemini to describe an image using several hundred words, and then fed the response text back to Gemini, prompting it to generate an image matching the description.\nL131: While this circular series of transformations does not satisfy differential privacy, it does illustrate the utility of text as an intermediary for synthetic image generation. As the saying goes, a picture is worth a thousand words — and it seems that it is not worth much more than that!\nL132: cite45†Image: Privately generated synthetic photo example†storage.googleapis.com L133: \nL134: Left: Original image. Right: Synthetic image.\nL135: \nL136: We asked Gemini to describe the original image in text, and then prompted Gemini to generate the synthetic image based on the text description.\nL137: \nL138: Concurrent work by cite46†Wang et al.†arxiv.org showed how one can leverage text-based intermediaries to generate differentially private single images using cite47†Private Evolution†arxiv.org .\nL139: ## Evaluation and results\nL140: We tested our method on the cite48†YFCC100M†arxiv.org dataset, a repository containing nearly 100 million images that have been released under the Creative Commons license. We formed “albums” from these images by grouping together photos taken by the same user within the same hour.\nL141: We constructed training sets for the large language models described above, taking care that no user contributes more than one example to any training set (contribution bounding is necessary to ensure the validity of the differential privacy guarantee).\nL142: After applying our method to generate synthetic photo albums, we evaluated how well they resemble the original albums. First, we computed the cite49†MAUVE score†arxiv.org , a neural embedding–based measure of semantic similarity, between the original and synthetic structured representations.\nL143: \nL144: The figure below shows the MAUVE scores between real and synthetic album summaries, as well as real and synthetic photo captions, both before and after fine-tuning.\nL145: cite50†Image: MAUVE scores between real and synthetic album summaries & captions†storage.googleapis.com L146: \nL147: Left: MAUVE scores between real and synthetic album summaries. Right: MAUVE scores between real and synthetic photo captions. Higher MAUVE scores indicate greater similarity. Higher values of the privacy parameter ε imply weaker privacy constraints.\nL148: Next, we calculated the most common topics in the album summaries, shown in the table below, and found that they were very similar between real and synthetic data.\nL149: \nL150: cite51†Image: Real album summaries vs synthetic album summaries†storage.googleapis.com L151: \nL152: Left: Most common topics in real album summaries. Right: Most common topics in synthetic album summaries.\nL153: Finally, direct visual examination of the synthetic photos albums shows that each album is typically centered on a common theme, just like real photo albums, as demonstrated by the examples in the figure below.\nL154: \nL155: cite52†Image: Privately generated synthetic photo albums†storage.googleapis.com L156: \nL157: Two synthetically-generated photo albums. Each album maintains a specific theme (top: apple picking trip; bottom: couple visits a meadow).\nL158: ## Conclusion\nL159: \nL160: The challenges of modern AI require data that is not only private, but also structurally and contextually rich, a need that simple, unstructured data can’t meet. By applying our hierarchical, text-as-intermediate method to the demanding task of generating coherent synthetic photo albums, we’ve successfully shown a pathway for extending the benefits of synthetic data beyond simple text or isolated images.\nL161: This methodology opens exciting new avenues for privacy-preserving AI innovation. It helps resolve the persistent tension between the need for large, high-quality data and the imperative to protect user privacy, paving the way for safer and more generalized AI development across critical industries.\nL162: ## Acknowledgements\nL163: \nL164: This work is the result of a collaboration between many people at Google Research, including (in alphabetical order by last name): Kareem Amin, Eva Bertels, Alex Bie, Rudrajit Das, Alessandro Epasto, Adel Javanmard, Weiwei Kong, Dennis Kraft, Alex Kurakin, Natalia Ponomareva, Monica Ribero, Jane Shapiro, Yurii Sushko, Umar Syed, and Sergei Vassilvitskii.\nL165: \nL166:   * Labels:\nL167:   * cite53†Generative AI L168: \nL169:   * cite54†Machine Intelligence L170: \nL171:   * cite55†Security, Privacy and Abuse Prevention L172: ## Quick links\nL173: \nL174: × ❮ ❯\nL175: \nL176: cite56†Image: PGSD-1-Cartoon†storage.googleapis.com L177: \nL178: Illustration of our method for generating synthetic photo albums.\nL179: \nL180: cite57†Image: PGSD-5-Albums†storage.googleapis.com L181: \nL182: Privately generated synthetic photo albums\nL183: \nL184: cite58†Image: PGSD-4-Topics†storage.googleapis.com L185: \nL186: Real album summaries vs synthetic album summaries\nL187: \nL188: cite59†Image: PGSD-2-Example†storage.googleapis.com L189: \nL190: Privately generated synthetic photo example\nL191: \nL192: cite60†Image: PGSD-3-Mauve†storage.googleapis.com L193: MAUVE scores between real and synthetic album summaries & captions\nL194: \nL195: ×\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTeaching Gemini to spot exploding stars with just a few examples (https://research.google/blog/teaching-gemini-to-spot-exploding-stars-with-just-a-few-examples/)\nciteturn2904view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2903view0\",\"id\":110}); Total lines: 169\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: ## Explore our many areas of focus\nL2: \nL3: cite1†Explore all research areas L4: \nL5: Applied AI & sciences\nL6: \nL7: cite2†Earth AI cite3†Health AI cite4†Science AI cite5†Sustainability & crisis resilience L8: \nL9: Foundational ML & algorithms\nL10: \nL11: cite6†Algorithms & theory cite7†Information retrieval cite8†Machine intelligence cite9†Machine perception cite10†Natural language processing L12: \nL13: People, systems & quantum AI\nL14: cite11†Human-computer interaction and visualization cite12†Networking cite13†Quantum AI cite14†Responsible AI cite15†Anti abuse cite16†Software engineering cite17†Software systems L15: \nL16: Learn More\nL17: \nL18: cite18†Publications cite19†Projects L19: ## Building a collaborative ecosystem\nL20: \nL21: cite20†Datasets Access high-quality datasets to accelerate your research. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. L22: \nL23: cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L24: ## Shaping the future together\nL25: \nL26: cite23†See all programs L27: \nL28: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. L29: \nL30: cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L31: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL32: \nL33: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. L34: \nL35: cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L36: \nL37: Research\nL38: ## Explore our many areas of focus\nL39: \nL40: cite1†Explore all research areas L41: \nL42: Applied AI & sciences\nL43: \nL44: cite2†Earth AI cite3†Health AI cite4†Science AI cite5†Sustainability & crisis resilience L45: \nL46: Foundational ML & algorithms\nL47: \nL48: cite6†Algorithms & theory cite7†Information retrieval cite8†Machine intelligence cite9†Machine perception cite10†Natural language processing L49: \nL50: People, systems & quantum AI\nL51: cite11†Human-computer interaction and visualization cite12†Networking cite13†Quantum AI cite14†Responsible AI cite15†Anti abuse cite16†Software engineering cite17†Software systems L52: \nL53: Learn More\nL54: \nL55: cite18†Publications cite19†Projects L56: \nL57: Resources\nL58: ## Building a collaborative ecosystem\nL59: \nL60: cite20†Datasets Access high-quality datasets to accelerate your research. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L61: \nL62: cite30†Conferences & events L63: \nL64: Careers\nL65: ## Shaping the future together\nL66: \nL67: cite23†See all programs L68: \nL69: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L70: \nL71: cite31†Blog L72: \nL73: About\nL74: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL75: \nL76: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L77: \nL78: cite32†Google Research L79: \nL80: cite33†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite34†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI†deepmind.google cite35†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google L81: \nL82: [Button: Research ]\nL83: \nL84: [Button: Resources ]\nL85: \nL86: cite30†Conferences & events L87: [Button: Careers ]\nL88: \nL89: cite31†Blog L90: \nL91: [Button: About ]\nL92: \nL93: [Input: Search]\nL94: \nL95: Search\nL96: \nL97: cite36†Image: Teaching Gemini to spot exploding stars with just a few examples†storage.googleapis.com L98: # Teaching Gemini to spot exploding stars with just a few examples\nL99: \nL100: October 20, 2025\nL101: \nL102: Turan Bulmus, Head of GenAI Blackbelts and Solution Architects, Google Cloud, and Dr. Fiorenzo Stoppa, Royal Society Newton International Fellow, University of Oxford\nL103: In a publication in Nature Astronomy, we show how Google's Gemini model can be transformed into an expert astronomy assistant that classifies cosmic events with high accuracy and explains its reasoning in plain language, achieving 93% accuracy across three datasets by learning from just 15 annotated examples per survey.\nL104: ## Quick links\nL105: \nL106:   * cite37†Paper†www.nature.com L107: Modern astronomy is a treasure hunt on a cosmic scale. Every night, telescopes around the globe scan the skies, searching for fleeting events like exploding stars (cite38†supernovae†en.wikipedia.org ) that give us crucial insights into the workings of the universe. These surveys generate millions of alerts about potential discoveries, but there’s a catch: the vast majority are not real cosmic events but \"bogus\" signals from satellite trails, cosmic ray hits, or other instrumental artefacts.\nL108: For years, astronomers have used specialized machine learning models, like cite39†convolutional neural networks†en.wikipedia.org (CNNs), to sift through this data. While effective, these models often act as “black boxes,” providing a simple \"real\" or \"bogus\" label with no explanation. This forces scientists to either blindly trust the output or spend countless hours manually verifying candidates — a bottleneck that will soon become insurmountable with next-generation telescopes like the cite40†Vera C.\nL109: Rubin Observatory†rubinobservatory.org , expected to generate cite41†10 million alerts per night†www.scientificamerican.com .\nL110: This challenge led us to ask a fundamental question: could a general-purpose, multimodal model, designed to understand text and images together, not only match the accuracy of these specialized models but also explain what it sees? In our paper, “cite37†Textual interpretation of transient image classifications from large language models†www.nature.com ”, published in cite42†Nature Astronomy†www.nature.com , we demonstrate that the answer is a resounding yes.\nL111: We show how Google’s Gemini model can be transformed into an expert astronomy assistant that can classify cosmic events with high accuracy and, crucially, explain its reasoning in plain language. We accomplished this by employing few-shot learning with Gemini, providing it with just 15 annotated examples per survey and concise instructions to accurately classify and explain cosmic events.\nL112: ## A new approach: Learning from a few examples\nL113: Instead of training a specialized model on millions of labeled images, we used a technique called cite43†few-shot learning†www.promptingguide.ai on a general-purpose model. We gave Gemini just 15 annotated examples for each of three major astronomical surveys: cite44†Pan-STARRS†en.wikipedia.org , cite45†MeerLICHT†science.uct.ac.za , and cite46†ATLAS†atlas.fallingstar.com .\nL114: Each example consisted of three small images: a new image of the transient alert, a reference image of the same patch of sky from a previous observation, and a difference image that highlights the change between the two.\nL115: Alongside these images, we provided a concise set of instructions, a short expert-written note explaining the classification, and an interest score (e.g., “high interest” for a likely supernova, “low interest” for a variable star, or “no interest” for a bogus signal) along with an explanation of that score.\nL116: The model had to learn to classify transients from a diverse set of telescopes, each with different resolutions, pixel scales, and camera characteristics. As shown below, the same celestial object can appear quite different across these surveys, but Gemini was able to generalize from the few examples provided.\nL117: \nL118: cite47†Image: ExplodingStars1_Surveys†storage.googleapis.com L119: Gemini operates across surveys with diverse pixel scales and resolutions. The same transient is observed in three different surveys, with rows corresponding to Pan-STARRS (top), MeerLICHT (middle) and ATLAS (bottom). Each row includes, from left to right, a new image, a reference image and a difference image.\nL120: The image stamps are all the same size in pixels (100 × 100) but differ in angular sky coverage due to survey-specific pixel scales: Pan-STARRS (0.25\" per pixel), MeerLICHT (0.56\" per pixel) and ATLAS (1.8\" per pixel).\nL121: Guided only by this minimal input, we asked Gemini to classify thousands of new alerts. The model achieved an average accuracy of 93% across the three datasets, which is on par with specialized CNNs that require massive, curated training datasets.\nL122: \nL123: But unlike a traditional classifier, we prompted Gemini not just to output a label but also to generate for every candidate:\nL124:   1. A textual explanation describing the features it observed and the logic behind its decision.\nL125:   2. An interest score to help astronomers prioritize follow-up observations.\nL126: \nL127: This turns the model from a black box into a transparent, interactive partner. Scientists can read the explanation to understand the model’s reasoning, building trust and allowing for more nuanced decision-making.\nL128: \nL129: cite48†Image: ExplodingStars2_Example†storage.googleapis.com L130: Gemini provides human-readable transient classifications and follow-up priorities. Each example consists of a new, reference and difference image for a candidate transient, followed by the Gemini classification, textual description and follow-up interest score. The examples shown in the figure are from the MeerLICHT dataset.\nL131: ## Knowing when to ask for help\nL132: A critical step in building a reliable system is ensuring the quality of its output. We assembled a panel of 12 professional astronomers who reviewed 200 of Gemini’s classifications and explanations.\nL133: Using a single, anchored 0–5 coherence rubric (0 = hallucination, 5 = perfectly coherent) tied to how well the text matched the new/reference/difference images, plus a simple Yes/Maybe/No check that the follow-up interest score agreed with the explanation, they rated the model’s descriptions as highly coherent and useful, confirming alignment with expert reasoning.\nL134: But perhaps our most important finding was that Gemini can effectively assess its own uncertainty. We prompted the model to assign a “coherence score” to its own explanations. We discovered that low-coherence scores were a powerful indicator of an incorrect classification. In other words, the model is good at telling us when it’s likely to be wrong. The details:\nL135: \nL136: cite49†Image: ExplodingStars3_Results†storage.googleapis.com L137: Left: Average coherence scores from 12 astronomers for 200 MeerLICHT transients, sorted by mean score (blue). Most examples received high values (4–5), indicating close alignment with user expectations. Inset: The consistency between the interest score assigned by the model & its own explanation, with nearly all cases marked as self-consistent (i.e., “Yes”). Right: Average user coherence scores, split by the correctness of the classification made by Gemini.\nL138: Correctly classified examples (TPs & TNs, green) tend to have higher coherence scores than incorrect ones (FPs & FNs, red).\nL139: This capability is a game-changer for building reliable \"human-in-the-loop\" workflows. By automatically flagging its most uncertain cases, the system can focus astronomers' attention where it is most needed. This creates a powerful feedback loop. By reviewing the flagged cases and adding a few of these challenging examples back into the prompt, we can rapidly improve the model’s performance.\nL140: Using this iterative process, we improved the model's accuracy on the MeerLICHT dataset from ~93.4% to ~96.7%, demonstrating how the system can learn and improve in partnership with human experts.\nL141: ## The future of scientific discovery\nL142: \nL143: We believe this approach marks a step toward a new era of scientific discovery — one accelerated by models that can both reason over complex scientific datasets and explain their outputs in natural language., but by models that can reason, explain their output, and collaborate with researchers.\nL144: Because this method requires only a small set of examples and plain-language instructions, it can potentially be rapidly adapted for new scientific instruments, surveys, and research goals across many different fields. We envision this technology as a foundation for \"agentic assistants\" in science. Such systems could integrate multiple data sources, check their own confidence, request follow-up observations, and escalate only the most promising discoveries to human scientists.\nL145: This work shows a path toward systems that learn with us, explain their reasoning, and empower researchers in any field to focus on what matters most: asking the next great question.\nL146: ## Acknowledgements\nL147: \nL148: This research was a collaborative effort. We extend our sincere thanks to our co-authors Steven Bloemen, Stephen J. Smartt, Paul J. Groot, Paul Vreeswijk, and Ken W. Smith.\nL149: \nL150:   * Labels:\nL151:   * cite50†General Science L152: \nL153:   * cite51†Generative AI L154: \nL155:   * cite52†Human-Computer Interaction and Visualization L156: ## Quick links\nL157: \nL158:   * cite37†Paper†www.nature.com L159: \nL160: × ❮ ❯\nL161: \nL162: cite53†Image: ExplodingStars2_Example†storage.googleapis.com L163: \nL164: cite54†Image: ExplodingStars3_Results†storage.googleapis.com L165: \nL166: cite36†Image: ExplodingStars1_Surveys†storage.googleapis.com L167: \nL168: ×\n"
